    "result_text": "The change is complete and the build is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe spec required all rejection responses (400, 409, 429) to return an RFC7807 `application/problem+json` body with `type`, `title`, `status`, and `detail`.\n\n**Root cause:** All owner-create rejections (400/409/429) flow through `OwnerRegistrationException`, but its handler in `ExceptionControllerAdvice.handleOwnerRegistrationException` returned a bodiless `ResponseEntity<Void>` \u2014 the status code was set but no problem+json body was emitted. (The bean-validation 400 path already returned a proper `ProblemDetail`.)\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. `OwnerRegistrationException.java` \u2014 gave each `Reason` a human-readable `detail` string (paired with its existing HTTP status), exposed via `getDetail()`, so every rejection names its own problem detail in one place alongside the status.\n\n2. `ExceptionControllerAdvice.java` \u2014 changed `handleOwnerRegistrationException` to return `ResponseEntity<ProblemDetail>`, building the body via the existing `detailBuild(...)` helper (which sets `type`, `title`, `status`, `detail`) with the reason's detail. This reuses the identical pattern already used by the 404/500/bean-validation handlers, so Spring serializes it as `application/problem+json` with the unchanged HTTP status.\n\n**Tests:** `./mvnw -o test` \u2192 `BUILD SUCCESS`, `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` (`AcceptanceTest.coreRejectionIsProblemJson` asserts the exact `application/problem+json` content type and `$.status == 400`). `AcceptanceTest` is the only concrete test class; the other files are an abstract base and a helper.",
